//==
// # Mesh geometry layers
//
// The layered vocabulary every mesh-like shape sits on: the vertex data as a
// view, the primitives as a view, and the indexed storage those primitives are
// gathered from. Layer 1 is the layer most algorithms should target — bounds,
// area, raycast, transform need to see primitives and nothing more, and soups,
// indexed meshes and tessellated procedural surfaces all satisfy it. Layer 2 is
// the storage layer, needed only by welding, adjacency and subdivision-class
// algorithms that must know which primitives share a vertex.
//
// Every layer is generic over the point type and the primitive, so the plane
// and space are the same tower instantiated twice: a triangulation is
// `IndexedGeometry<Point2D, Triangle2D, TriangleFace>` and an interchange mesh
// is `IndexedGeometry<Point3D, Triangle3D, TriangleFace>`. A UV domain is the
// planar instantiation sharing its index table with the spatial one. There are
// deliberately no named per-primitive aliases (no `TriangleGeometry3D`): each
// would abstract over a single concrete type, which is a second spelling of
// that type rather than an abstraction, and would collide with the type names
// in `meshes.types.plato`. Functions constrain on the generic concept with
// concrete arguments instead.
//
// Manifoldness, closedness and orientability are deliberately absent: they are
// structural markers in `geometry.concepts.plato`, bolted on per type the way
// `ClosedCurve2D` marks a curve, not members of this tower. Element counts and
// incidence live in `topology.concepts.plato`.
// Convention: see CONVENTIONS.md - Winding, handedness, and normals.

// Exposes the points a shape is built from, in a fixed order, generic over the
// embedding. The dimension-agnostic root of the tower: `PointSet2D` /
// `PointSet3D` (`geometry-measures.concepts.plato`) are the non-generic
// siblings that carry the Geometry2D / Geometry3D marker with them.
concept PointGeometry<TPoint>
    inherits Geometry
{
    Points(x: Self): Array<TPoint>;
}

// Exposes the whole primitives a shape enumerates — segments, triangles, quads
// — alongside the points inside them. The view layer: an algorithm that only
// folds over primitives constrains on this and works for unwelded soups,
// indexed meshes and tessellated surfaces alike.
concept PrimitiveGeometry<TPoint, TPrim>
    inherits PointGeometry<TPoint>
{
    Primitives(x: Self): Array<TPrim>;
}

// Primitives stored as index tuples into a shared vertex pool: the storage
// layer under an indexed mesh (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polygon_mesh).
// Every index must be valid — no -1 inside a face. TIndex is the typed index
// tuple that fixes the primitive's arity — VertexPair, TriangleFace, QuadFace —
// so the pairing of primitive to arity needs no separate name to hold it.
// `Primitives` is derivable here (gather Vertices by FaceIndices), so an
// implementor supplies the pool and the index table and inherits the view.
concept IndexedGeometry<TPoint, TPrim, TIndex>
    inherits PrimitiveGeometry<TPoint, TPrim>
{
    Vertices(x: Self): Array<TPoint>;
    FaceIndices(x: Self): Array<TIndex>;
}

// A finite ordered group of whole geometric elements treated as one shape:
// scene parts, connected components, a batch of meshes drawn together. The
// elements keep their own type; the collection adds no geometry of its own.
concept GeometryCollection<TGeom>
    inherits Geometry
{
    Elements(x: Self): Array<TGeom>;
}
